From f97b316c4761cb4ebc4d3df78243b278465663d8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Remora <remora@localhost>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 22:27:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09/16] venus: publish display frames to a host encoder over a
 socket
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The res_id chain identifies which buffer holds the current display frame; this
hands that buffer to a separate encoder process, passing the dma_buf itself by
SCM_RIGHTS so the pixels never leave the GPU.

One message per display frame — magic, res_id, extent, has_fd — with the fd sent
once per resource and cached by the receiver, since the display rotates through a
fixed pool. sendmsg() is MSG_DONTWAIT: this runs on the submit path, so a stalled
encoder costs a dropped notification, never a blocked compositor.

Two things measured while validating the transport, both of which produced a
working-looking socket that delivered the wrong thing:

A resource id does not mean one buffer for the life of the server — virgl
RECYCLES ids. Keeping the first fd seen for an id made two of four display
res_ids hand back 65536-byte fds, the size of a 120x120 icon that had previously
held the id, in place of the 30 MiB frame. The newest import wins, and the
"already sent" record is cleared with it so a connected encoder is re-sent the
live fd rather than caching a dead one.

The ENCODER listens and the render server connects, not the reverse. Venus forks
a render server per client connection (measured: 11 on a live session) and each
composites for a different guest process; an app drawing a full-screen surface
reports the display's extent, so no fork can tell on its own whether it owns the
screen. With the server listening they fought over one socket path — each new
fork unlinked the incumbent and bound its own, so the name resolved to whoever
bound last while the rest listened on orphaned inodes, and an encoder connected
successfully to a process that never rendered another frame. Connecting removes
the contention and makes the choice decidable, because only the encoder sees
every candidate at once. It also sidesteps res_id ambiguity: ids are per-context,
so all three publishers in a live session report res_id=4 for different buffers.
